---

<DocsLayout
  title="Plugin recipes"
  subtitle="Eight complete plugins that each do one thing. Copy one, change the parts that are yours, point nikcli.json at the file."
  description="Copy-pasteable nikcli plugin examples: custom tools, progress streaming, permission policy, shell secrets, model parameters, request redaction, system prompt rules and notifications."
  toc={toc}
>
  <section id="how" class="doc-section">
    <h2>How to use these</h2>
    <p>
      Each recipe is a whole file. Drop it in <code>.nikcli/plugins/</code>, add the path to
      <code>plugin</code> in <code>nikcli.json</code>, and restart. If any of that is unfamiliar,
      read <a href="/docs/guides/plugins">Writing a plugin</a> first.
    </p>
    <DocCode language="json" title="nikcli.json">{`{
  "$schema": "https://nikcli-ai.dev/config.json",
  "plugin": ["./.nikcli/plugins/deploy.ts"]
}`}</DocCode>
    <DocCallout type="warn" title="These run with your credentials">
      <p>
        A plugin has the access the agent has. Read a recipe before running it, the same way you
        would read a shell script someone handed you.
      </p>
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

  <section id="tool" class="doc-section">
    <h2>1. A tool that runs a script</h2>
    <p>
      The most common reason to write a plugin: give the agent a verb it cannot get wrong, instead
      of hoping it assembles the right shell command.
    </p>
    <DocCode language="ts" title=".nikcli/plugins/deploy.ts">{`import type { Plugin } from "@nikcli-ai/plugin"
import { tool } from "@nikcli-ai/plugin/tool"

export const Deploy: Plugin = async ({ $ }) => ({
  tool: {
    deploy: tool({
      description: "Deploy a branch to staging. Use this instead of running deploy scripts by hand.",
      args: {
        branch: tool.schema.string().describe("branch to deploy, e.g. feature/login"),
        dryRun: tool.schema.boolean().optional().describe("print the plan without applying it"),
      },
      async execute(args, ctx) {
        ctx.metadata({ title: \`Deploying \${args.branch}\` })

        const result = await $\`./script/deploy.sh \${args.branch} \${args.dryRun ? "--dry-run" : ""}\`
          .nothrow()
          .quiet()

        if (result.exitCode !== 0) {
          return { title: "Deploy failed", output: result.stderr.toString() }
        }
        return { title: \`Deployed \${args.branch}\`, output: result.stdout.toString() }
      },
    }),
  },
})`}</DocCode>
  </section>

  <section id="progress" class="doc-section">
    <h2>2. A tool that streams progress</h2>
    <p>
      Long-running tools should report as they go. <code>ctx.progress</code> pushes partial results
      before the tool returns, and <code>ctx.abort</code> lets the user cancel it.
    </p>
    <DocCode language="ts" title=".nikcli/plugins/migrate.ts">{`import type { Plugin } from "@nikcli-ai/plugin"
import { tool } from "@nikcli-ai/plugin/tool"

export const Migrate: Plugin = async ({ directory }) => ({
  tool: {
    migrate: tool({
      description: "Run pending database migrations one at a time",
      args: { to: tool.schema.string().optional().describe("target migration id") },
      async execute(args, ctx) {
        const pending = await findPending(directory, args.to)
        const applied: string[] = []

        for (const [index, migration] of pending.entries()) {
          if (ctx.abort.aborted) break

          await ctx.progress({
            structured: { done: index, total: pending.length, current: migration },
            content: [{ type: "text", text: \`Applying \${migration}…\` }],
          })

          await apply(migration, { signal: ctx.abort })
          applied.push(migration)
        }

        return {
          title: \`\${applied.length} migration(s) applied\`,
          output: applied.join("\\n") || "nothing to do",
          metadata: { applied },
        }
      },
    }),
  },
})`}</DocCode>
  </section>

  <section id="policy" class="doc-section">
    <h2>3. Auto-deny a permission class</h2>
    <p>
      <code>permission.ask</code> runs before the user is prompted. Setting
      <code>output.status</code> decides it without them: <code>deny</code> refuses,
      <code>allow</code> approves silently, and leaving it as <code>ask</code> falls through to the
      normal prompt.
    </p>
    <DocCode language="ts" title=".nikcli/plugins/policy.ts">{`import type { Plugin } from "@nikcli-ai/plugin"

const FORBIDDEN = [/\\bgit\\s+push\\s+--force/, /\\brm\\s+-rf\\s+\\//, /\\bcurl\\b.*\\|\\s*sh/]

export const Policy: Plugin = async () => ({
  async "permission.ask"(request, output) {
    const target = (request.patterns ?? []).join(" ")

    if (FORBIDDEN.some((pattern) => pattern.test(target))) {
      output.status = "deny"
      return
    }

    // read-only lookups never need a prompt
    if (request.permission === "webfetch") output.status = "allow"
  },
})`}</DocCode>
    <DocCallout type="info" title="This composes with permission modes">
      <p>
        The hook narrows what config already allows — it cannot widen past a mode that denies
        outright. See <a href="/docs/permissions">Permissions</a>.
      </p>
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

  <section id="secrets" class="doc-section">
    <h2>4. Inject secrets into the shell</h2>
    <p>
      <code>shell.env</code> adds environment variables to every PTY and shell the server creates,
      so commands can authenticate without the value ever appearing in a transcript.
    </p>
    <DocCode language="ts" title=".nikcli/plugins/shell-env.ts">{`import type { Plugin } from "@nikcli-ai/plugin"

export const ShellEnv: Plugin = async () => ({
  async "shell.env"(input, output) {
    output.env.NPM_TOKEN = process.env.CI_NPM_TOKEN ?? ""
    output.env.DATABASE_URL = \`postgres://localhost/app_dev\`

    // only inside the infra checkout
    if (input.cwd.includes("/infra")) {
      output.env.AWS_PROFILE = "staging"
    }
  },
})`}</DocCode>
    <p>
      Values are merged over the caller's environment, then terminal invariants such as
      <code>TERM</code> and <code>NIKCLI_TERMINAL</code> are re-applied, so you cannot break the
      terminal by accident.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="params" class="doc-section">
    <h2>5. Tune model parameters</h2>
    <p>
      <code>chat.params</code> runs before every model call and knows which agent is running, so
      sampling can differ per role.
    </p>
    <DocCode language="ts" title=".nikcli/plugins/sampling.ts">{`import type { Plugin } from "@nikcli-ai/plugin"

export const Sampling: Plugin = async () => ({
  async "chat.params"(input, output) {
    // deterministic for review work, looser for brainstorming
    if (input.agent === "review") {
      output.temperature = 0
      output.topP = 1
      return
    }
    if (input.agent === "plan") output.temperature = 0.7

    // provider-specific knobs go in options
    if (input.provider.info.id === "anthropic") {
      output.options.max_tokens = 8192
    }
  },
})`}</DocCode>
  </section>

  <section id="redact" class="doc-section">
    <h2>6. Redact outbound requests</h2>
    <p>
      <code>chat.request</code> is the last hook before the HTTP request leaves the process. It sees
      the serialized <code>url</code>, <code>headers</code> and <code>body</code> — which makes it
      the right place for a redaction pass, and the wrong place for anything slow.
    </p>
    <DocCode language="ts" title=".nikcli/plugins/redact.ts">{`import type { Plugin } from "@nikcli-ai/plugin"

const PATTERNS: Array<[RegExp, string]> = [
  [/sk-[A-Za-z0-9]{20,}/g, "[redacted-api-key]"],
  [/-----BEGIN [A-Z ]*PRIVATE KEY-----[\\s\\S]*?-----END [A-Z ]*PRIVATE KEY-----/g, "[redacted-key]"],
]

export const Redact: Plugin = async () => ({
  async "chat.request"(_input, output) {
    if (!output.body) return
    let body = output.body
    for (const [pattern, replacement] of PATTERNS) body = body.replace(pattern, replacement)
    output.body = body
  },
})`}</DocCode>
    <DocCallout type="warn" title="Redaction here is a net, not a wall">
      <p>
        It catches what its patterns match on the way out. Keep secrets out of the context in the
        first place — this is the last line, not the only one.
      </p>
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

  <section id="system" class="doc-section">
    <h2>7. Append project rules</h2>
    <p>
      <code>experimental.chat.system.transform</code> receives the system prompt as an array of
      blocks. Pushing onto it adds house rules to every turn without editing a prompt file.
    </p>
    <DocCode language="ts" title=".nikcli/plugins/house-rules.ts">{`import type { Plugin } from "@nikcli-ai/plugin"
import { readFile } from "node:fs/promises"
import path from "node:path"

export const HouseRules: Plugin = async ({ directory }) => {
  const rules = await readFile(path.join(directory, "RULES.md"), "utf8").catch(() => "")

  return {
    async "experimental.chat.system.transform"(_input, output) {
      if (rules) output.system.push(rules)
    },
  }
}`}</DocCode>
    <p>
      Reading the file once at load rather than per turn matters: this hook is on the hot path of
      every model call.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="notify" class="doc-section">
    <h2>8. Notify on session idle</h2>
    <p>
      The <code>event</code> hook sees every server event. <code>session.idle</code> is the one that
      means a turn finished — useful when a long task is running in another window.
    </p>
    <DocCode language="ts" title=".nikcli/plugins/slack-notify.ts">{`import type { Plugin } from "@nikcli-ai/plugin"

type Options = { webhook?: string }

export const SlackNotify: Plugin = async ({ client }, options) => {
  const webhook = (options as Options | undefined)?.webhook ?? process.env.SLACK_WEBHOOK
  if (!webhook) return {}

  return {
    async event({ event }) {
      if (event.type !== "session.idle") return

      const { data: session } = await client.session.get({
        sessionID: event.properties.sessionID,
      })

      await fetch(webhook, {
        method: "POST",
        headers: { "content-type": "application/json" },
        body: JSON.stringify({ text: \`nikcli finished: \${session?.title ?? "session"}\` }),
      })
    },
  }
}`}</DocCode>
    <p>
      Every event type is listed in the <code>Event</code> union the SDK exports — among them
      <code>session.error</code>, <code>permission.asked</code>, <code>question.asked</code>,
      <code>file.edited</code> and <code>todo.updated</code>.
    </p>
    <DocCallout type="note" title="nikcli already ships notifications">
      <p>
        Before writing this one, check <a href="/docs/plugins#notifications">the built-in notify
        plugin</a> — it covers macOS, Slack and Discord with rate limiting and a circuit breaker.
      </p>
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

  <DocRelated
    links={[
      { title: "Writing a plugin", href: "/docs/guides/plugins", description: "The guide these recipes assume you have read." },
      { title: "Permissions", href: "/docs/permissions", description: "What permission.ask composes with." },
      { title: "Tools", href: "/docs/tools", description: "The built-in tools, and how gating decides what the model sees." },
      { title: "API Reference", href: "/docs/api", description: "Everything the plugin's client can call." },
    ]}
  />
</DocsLayout>
